    <h1>Fetal Development: Week by Week</h1>

    <p>Fetal development is a remarkable process. In just 40 weeks, a single cell grows into a fully formed baby. Here is what happens at each stage, sourced to ACOG, NICHD, and Mayo Clinic.</p>

    <h2>Embryo Versus Fetus: When the Name Changes</h2>

    <p>The embryonic period runs for the first eight weeks after fertilisation. After that the baby is called a fetus. Conception usually happens about two weeks after the first day of the last period, so eight weeks after fertilisation falls at week 10 of pregnancy as normally counted.</p>

    <p>Mayo Clinic places the switch between eight and nine weeks after conception: still an embryo at week 10, a fetus at week 11.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, Fetal development: The first trimester, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/pregnancy-week-by-week/in-depth/prenatal-care/art-20045302</em></p>

    <h2>Weeks 1-4: Conception and Implantation</h2>

    <p>Fertilization occurs when a sperm meets an egg, usually in the fallopian tube. The fertilized egg (zygote) divides rapidly as it travels toward the uterus. By week 4, the blastocyst implants in the uterine lining.</p>

    <p>At this stage, you may not know you are pregnant. Some women experience light spotting (implantation bleeding), but many have no symptoms.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/pregnancy-week-by-week/in-depth/prenatal-care/art-20045302; NICHD, https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pregnancy/conditioninfo</em></p>

    <h2>Weeks 5-8: The Embryonic Period</h2>

    <p>This is the most critical period of development. All major organs and structures begin to form.</p>

    <h3>Week 5</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Neural tube forms (becomes brain and spinal cord)</li>
      <li>Heart begins to beat</li>
      <li>Embryo is about the size of a sesame seed</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 6</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Heart is beating regularly</li>
      <li>Arm and leg buds appear</li>
      <li>Eyes and ears begin to form</li>
      <li>Embryo is about the size of a lentil</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 7</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Brain grows rapidly</li>
      <li>Facial features become more defined</li>
      <li>Arms and legs grow longer</li>
      <li>Embryo is about the size of a blueberry</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 8</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>All major organs have begun to form</li>
      <li>Fingers and toes are visible</li>
      <li>Tail disappears</li>
      <li>Embryo is about the size of a kidney bean</li>
    </ul>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h2>Organogenesis: When Each Structure Forms</h2>

    <p>Organogenesis is the name for the weeks when organs are laid down. Mayo Clinic gives the order.</p>

    <p>At week 6 of pregnancy, or four weeks after conception, the neural tube along the back closes and the brain and spinal cord develop from it. The heart and other organs start to form at the same time.</p>

    <p>At week 7, or five weeks after conception, the brain and face grow, the first part of the retinas forms, lower limb buds appear and the arm buds take the shape of paddles.</p>

    <p>At week 8, or six weeks after conception, fingers have begun to form, the ears and eyes become noticeable and the upper lip and nose have formed. The embryo is about half an inch, 11 to 14 mm, from crown to rump. By week 10, or eight weeks after conception, the fingers and toes have lost their webbing and grown longer.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, Fetal development: The first trimester, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/pregnancy-week-by-week/in-depth/prenatal-care/art-20045302</em></p>

    <h2>When the Heart Starts to Beat</h2>

    <p>The heart is one of the first structures to form. Mayo Clinic dates the start of heart formation, and of the other organs, to week 6 of pregnancy, or four weeks after conception.</p>

    <p>The authorities used for this page do not give one agreed week for when cardiac activity first becomes visible on a scan, so no specific week is stated here. Ask your clinician about your own scan.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, Fetal development: The first trimester, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/pregnancy-week-by-week/in-depth/prenatal-care/art-20045302</em></p>

    <h2>Weeks 9-13: Fetal Period Begins</h2>

    <p>ACOG states that for 8 weeks after fertilization the developing baby is called an embryo, and from 9 weeks after fertilization until birth it is called a fetus. Because pregnancy is dated from the last menstrual period, 9 weeks after fertilization is 11 weeks of pregnancy. By the end of the first trimester, all major organs have formed.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG, How Your Fetus Grows During Pregnancy, https://www.acog.org/patient-resources/faqs/pregnancy/how-your-fetus-grows-during-pregnancy</em></p>

    <h3>Week 9</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fetus is about the size of a grape</li>
      <li>Eyelids form</li>
      <li>Genitalia begin to differentiate</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 10</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fetus is about the size of a strawberry</li>
      <li>Fingernails and toenails begin to form</li>
      <li>Baby can make facial expressions</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 11</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fetus is about the size of a lime</li>
      <li>All major organs have formed</li>
      <li>Baby can swallow and urinate</li>
      <li>Embryo → fetus transition (ACOG)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 12</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fetus is about the size of a plum</li>
      <li>Reflexes develop</li>
      <li>Baby can make a fist</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 13</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fetus is about the size of a lemon</li>
      <li>Intestines move into the abdomen</li>
      <li>Vocal cords develop</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/pregnancy-week-by-week/in-depth/prenatal-care/art-20045302</em></p>

    <h2>Weeks 14-27: Second Trimester</h2>

    <p>The second trimester is a period of rapid growth and maturation. The fetus grows from about 3.5 inches to about 12 inches.</p>

    <h3>Week 14-16</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby's neck gets longer</li>
      <li>Baby can make facial expressions</li>
      <li>Baby's sex may be visible on ultrasound</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 17-20</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Quickening (first movement) typically 16-22 weeks</li>
      <li>Baby can hear sounds</li>
      <li>Baby's skin is covered with vernix</li>
      <li>Anatomy scan at 18-22 weeks</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 21-24</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby's lungs are developing</li>
      <li>Baby has sleep-wake cycles</li>
      <li>Baby can respond to sounds</li>
      <li>Periviable period: 20 0/7 to 25 6/7 weeks</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 25-27</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby's eyes can open</li>
      <li>Baby can respond to light</li>
      <li>Baby's brain is developing rapidly</li>
      <li>Baby is practicing breathing movements</li>
    </ul>

    <p>NICHD notes that a woman may begin to feel movement at 20 weeks, and that between 18 and 20 weeks an ultrasound is usually done to look for congenital anomalies.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NICHD, https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pregnancy/conditioninfo; NHS, Week-by-week guide to pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/week-by-week/</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h2>Weeks 28-40: Third Trimester</h2>

    <p>The NHS splits pregnancy into a first trimester of weeks 4 to 12, a second of weeks 13 to 27 and a third of weeks 28 to 41. This is the final stretch, when growth in length slows and the organs finish maturing.</p>

    <h3>Week 28-32</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby is about 15 inches long</li>
      <li>Baby weighs about 2.5 pounds</li>
      <li>Baby can open and close eyes</li>
      <li>Baby's bones are fully formed but soft</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 33-36</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby is about 17-18.5 inches long</li>
      <li>Baby weighs about 4-6 pounds</li>
      <li>Baby's lungs are nearly mature</li>
      <li>Baby is usually head-down</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Week 37-40</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby is full term (37 0/7 weeks)</li>
      <li>Baby's organs are fully mature</li>
      <li>Baby is about 18.5-20 inches long</li>
      <li>Baby weighs about 6-7.5 pounds (average varies widely)</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Week-by-week guide to pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/week-by-week/; NICHD, https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pregnancy/conditioninfo</em></p>

    <h2>Lung Maturity in the Third Trimester</h2>

    <p>The lungs are among the last organs to finish developing. NHS guidance states that by week 36 the baby's lungs are probably mature enough to breathe outside the womb without help.</p>

    <p>NICHD advises that, where there is no medical reason to deliver earlier, delivery at or after 39 weeks gives the infant's lungs, brain and liver time to fully develop.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, You and your baby at 36 weeks pregnant, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/3rd-trimester/week-36/; NICHD, https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pregnancy/conditioninfo</em></p>

    <h2>Third-Trimester Brain Development</h2>

    <p>NICHD describes the baby at 32 weeks as having bones that are soft yet almost fully formed, and eyes that can open and close. The brain keeps developing to the end of pregnancy, which is why NICHD points to 39 or 40 weeks as full term, with better health outcomes than earlier birth.</p>

    <p>Infants born before 37 weeks are preterm, and NICHD lists developmental delays, vision and hearing problems and cerebral palsy among the risks. NHS guidance gives a length of about 47.4 cm from head to heel at week 36.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NICHD, https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pregnancy/conditioninfo; NHS, You and your baby at 36 weeks pregnant, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/3rd-trimester/week-36/</em></p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You have questions about fetal development</li>
      <li>You have not felt movement by 24 weeks</li>
      <li>You have bleeding, pain, or other concerns</li>
      <li>You are past your due date</li>
    </ul>

